## 5. Draft the GitHub issue, check the tone, show both
- **Title:** imperative and actionable (what to do, not the topic), at most 72 characters.
- **Description:** follow the writing standard and `{{ issue_template }}` — a guide, not a form: keep
  its sections in their order and what each is for, adapt the wording to the topic, and leave its
  `<!-- -->` comments out (if it is missing, use: Intent with the **Blocks prod?** and **Depends on the
  platform view** lines, Options and trade-offs including "do nothing", Proposed call, Needs from the
  approvers, and the Plan in a collapsed `<details>` block). At most {{ max_words }} words — state the
  word count. Link the intent file when there is one. Short as it is, it hides no flaw: each one of
  Medium or higher gets at least a line — in the Intent, or in an option's "what can go wrong" —
  with its trigger, impact, severity, and its evidence or a link to it in the intent file (with no
  intent file, its full evidence). Leave the Plan as the template's outline
  until the intent is agreed (step 6).{% if adversary %} Name every UNRESOLVED adversary finding, with its evidence, so the
  approvers decide with it in view.{% endif %}
- **One line per paragraph:** GitHub shows every newline in an issue as a line break, so write each
  paragraph and each list item of the description on one line — never wrap it by hand. Break a line
  only where the reader should see one, like the **Blocks prod?** and **Depends on the platform
  view** lines. ocgen flags lines wrapped mid-sentence after every write to the draft.
- **Needs from the approvers:** {% if approvers %}name them in that heading ({{ approvers }}), then one pending checklist
  line per approver, as @mentions — GitHub notifies them when I file the issue{% else %}"No approvers configured"{% endif %}. That is the only
  notice: **Never email**, message or otherwise contact anyone. Don't use `--assignee` for approvers —
  assignees do the work.
{% include "intent/tone-check.md" %}
  Fix everything it reports. Then write the intent file (if there is one), and write the description with the Write tool
